ong class='sig'>\u22120.14\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.20 to \u22120.07), about \u22122.0\u00b0F since 1875. Pittsburgh's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.05\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.11 to +0.01) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.59\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.25 to +0.94) and highs show no clear change. The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 8 months fell. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months; January and July fell.","t_rain":"Pittsburgh has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Pittsburgh's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.28 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.09 to +0.48), about +4.4 inches since 1871. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 39.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 2018 (57.83 in); the driest, 1930 (22.65 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 4 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}